    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;"><h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">LESSON IDENTIFICATION</h2></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Rooster!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can draw a simple picture and label it with one or two familiar words with teacher support (e.g., 'cat' or 'dog'). Can engage in interactive labeling activities by labeling pictures of animals and their sounds (e.g., write 'cow' and 'moo' under a picture of a cow). Can label drawings of animals created by peers with correct information (e.g., add 'fish' next to a peer's drawing of a fish).</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h3></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h3></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h3></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <!-- STAGE 1 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "What's that Sound?" TPR activity. The teacher says an animal sound (e.g., "Moo!"), and students mimic the sound and make the animal's motion (e.g., imitate a cow). The teacher then shows flashcards of the animal and asks, "What animal is this?" (e.g., "cow"). This reviews familiar animal names and sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large flashcard showing a rooster. The teacher points to the picture and says, "That's a rooster! A rooster says 'cock-a-doodle-doo'!" (while making the sound). The teacher then shows the word 'rooster' written clearly on a card, tracing the letters with a finger and saying, "R-O-O-S-T-E-R. Rooster." The teacher models the pronunciation of /r/ in 'rooster'. The teacher also shows a 'cat' flashcard and the word 'cat', explicitly sounding out 'C-A-T, cat' and modeling /m/ in 'meow'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of a cat: "Is this a rooster or a cat?" (Expected answer: "A cat!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher makes a 'woof' sound: "What sound is this? 'Woof woof'!" (Expected answer: "Dog!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 2 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "What's that Sound?":</b> The teacher uses a digital presentation or large flashcards to introduce written animal names and their sounds. Each slide/card features a clear picture of an animal, its name written clearly below it, and a thought bubble showing its sound. The teacher presents: 'pig' ('oink'), 'duck' ('quack'), 'horse' ('neigh'), 'sheep' ('baa'). Explicitly introduces the letters P, Q, R by highlighting them in the words 'pig', 'quack', 'rooster'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated digital presentation or large picture flashcards with written animal names (e.g., 'pig', 'duck', 'horse', 'sheep') and their associated sounds. Focus is on visual recognition of the written words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying and describing different animals based on their sounds, and recognizing the written form of their names and sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Game:</b> The teacher shows a picture of an animal and holds up three written word cards (e.g., 'pig', 'cat', 'dog'). Students point to the correct written animal name.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Sound to Word":</b> The teacher makes an animal sound (e.g., "Quack!"), and students point to the corresponding written animal name ('duck') or sound ('quack') on a poster board.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 3 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing target animal names and their sounds.
                    <br><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple ('The cat says meow.'), Exclamations ('That's a rooster!').
                    <br><b>Vocabulary:</b> cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, fish, pig, bark, meow, quack, woof.
                    <br><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Recognizing letters of the alphabet in animal names (C, D, P, Q, R).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing Animal Names:</b> Students receive a worksheet with large dotted outlines of animal names (e.g., 'cat', 'dog', 'pig', 'duck') and their sounds ('meow', 'woof', 'oink', 'quack'). They trace the letters with crayons or pencils. The teacher circulates, providing support and reminding students of letter formation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture-Word Matching & Copying:</b> Students are given another worksheet with pictures of familiar animals on one side and a word bank (e.g., 'cow', 'moo', 'rooster', 'bark', 'bird', 'chirp') on the other. Students draw a line from each picture to its correct name and then copy the name next to the picture. They also copy the corresponding sound. For example, next to a cow picture, they copy 'cow' and 'moo'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Labeling Check:</b> In pairs, students exchange their completed worksheets. Each student points to an animal on their partner's sheet and asks, "What animal is this?" or "What sound is this?" The partner responds verbally and then points to the written label. They collaboratively check if the labels are correct. The teacher models: "Is this a 'pig'? What does the pig say?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 4 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Fluency focus)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "My Animal Friend and Its Sound!"</b> Students will create a simple drawing and label it with the animal's name and its sound, applying the writing skills practiced. This activity is directly connected to the theme "That's a Rooster!" and scenario "What's that Sound?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creative drawing and labeling activity followed by peer labeling.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: My Animal Drawing:</b> Each student receives a blank sheet of paper. The teacher instructs them: "Draw your favorite animal from our lesson! Big or small. It can be a cat, a dog, a rooster, a pig... any animal!" (Teacher holds up visual cues of animals). After drawing, students are prompted to label their drawing. The teacher provides a 'word bank' poster on the board with written animal names (e.g., CAT, DOG, ROOSTER, PIG) and their sounds (MEOW, WOOF, OINK, COCK-A-DOODLE-DOO) for support. Students choose their animal's name and its sound from the word bank and carefully copy them next to their drawing. Example: A student draws a rooster and writes 'Rooster' and 'Cock-a-doodle-doo'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Peer Labeling:</b> Students then swap their drawings with a partner. Each student looks at their peer's drawing and tries to read the label. If the label is missing or they want to add more, they can write another familiar word (e.g., 'big', 'small') or another animal sound (e.g., if a peer drew a fish, they might add 'fish' or 'swim' next to it, using the word bank for reference).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 5 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and simple Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their animal drawings with their labels and any peer additions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can draw a simple picture of an animal.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can copy/label their drawing with the correct animal name (e.g., 'cat').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can copy/label their drawing with the correct animal sound (e.g., 'meow').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to label peer drawings with correct information (e.g., 'fish').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in letter formation and copying.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students to show a thumbs up if they can draw and write an animal name, and a thumbs down if they need more practice. Then, each student draws a happy face or a sad face on their paper indicating how they feel about their writing today. The teacher collects the papers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <!-- STAGE 6 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite animal to draw and label today?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or tricky to write the animal names?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully copied animal names and sounds, demonstrating an understanding of basic labeling. The tracing and copying activities were effective in building foundational writing skills for this age group. Some students needed more individual support with letter formation, especially for 'P', 'Q', and 'R'. For the next lesson, more guided practice on these specific letters and reinforcing the connection between letter shapes and sounds might be beneficial. Perhaps pre-writing lines or tracing patterns before writing words. The peer labeling encouraged interaction but needs clear instructions for Kinder. The word bank was essential support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will consolidate vocabulary and sounds by having students present their labeled drawings to the class and explain them to a partner who might need help understanding (mediation), leading to the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>